 the good we can do a lot of good and  help a lot of babies by commencing dried  blood spot screening with our current  methodologies  but these need to be constantly looked  at  and new and better approaches will come  forth in the future and that's been true  for all screenable disorders  in newborn screening  continuing to look at the costs is going  to be critical and then reflecting how  this  will become a part of  what is arguably a changing landscape in  newborn screening  in the advent of the molecular era  uh what what other changes might we  anticipate for other potentially  screenable disorders  that will be an interesting discussion  to participate in in the future as we  move forward  so with that i want to stop uh and  look forward to hearing your questions  uh  this slide is sadly not comprehensive  but um it does capture  at least a lot of the people who've  played critical roles in these  collaborations including  sheila dollard  at the cdc tatiana lanzieri also at the  cdc  mark mccann the director of the newbern  screening program at the state health  department in minnesota and sandra  rosendahl who's been  the genetic counselor and director  of notification  for  newborn screening results kirsten  coverstone in our eddie department  laboratory staff and personnel our state  epidemiologist a pediatrician ruth  linfield who's been very supportive of  these studies  this has been a study not only done in  our university of minnesota healthcare  system but  allina health which is another health  system in minnesota abby sidebottom  who's the director of research there  then people in my own group and  laboratory  shown here in front of the laboratory at  the university of minnesota  and  listed over here on the right side of  the slide  incomplete but at least a  reasonable list of all of the  collaborators and  ancillary staff who've helped  make this work possible  so i think with that um we will move on  into  questions  and i look forward to  hearing your questions and and comments  thank you dr schleich for your  informative presentation we will now  start the live q a portion of the  webinar if you have a question you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the ask a question box located on the  far left of your screen we'll answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for  all right let's get started our first  question here asks there are some  commercial platforms available that do  rapid bedside testing of saliva to look  for cmv dna could these be used for  universal congenital cmv screening  yeah i mean it's such an important  question as we see these platforms now  some of them are licensed and marketed  how how should they be used and i think  that  there is a role for these  targeted screening of babies that refer  the new word hearing screen would be one  such role  whether these would be adopt adaptable  to universal screening  i think we'll see i mean this is one of  the deliberations that the minnesota  department of health newborn screening  program will be  discussing as they develop an  implementation plan  i've been an advocate for the dried  blood spot because  i believe it has  a clinically  tenable  sensitivity  and a better specificity that is to say  fewer false positives as one might see  with saliva  my other concern about some of the  platforms that are being marketed for  saliva testing  could be their cost and this could  circle back to health equity issues  uh in the state health department  there's a premium on finding a sensitive  test at a  minimum cost so that it's available to  everybody  and um i think that  the cost of  the bedside  fee for service  marketed platforms um is something we  need to consider moving forward but it's  a great question and uh i again i like  the idea of dried blood spots for the  other reason  being that they're collected anyway i  mean we get them on all newborns and so  it's much less of a cost to the system  because let's face it in the united  states of america the most costly thing  in healthcare  is cost of people's time  and so the additional time required for  nursing staff to  obtain  additional specimens whether it's saliva  or urine  is a concern that needs to be considered  thank you  next question here should a baby that  screams positive for cmv be treated with  anti-viral drugs like valganciclavir  yes well that thank you for that  question i i think that circles back to  some of the comments i was trying to  make about the concern about the overuse  of ganciclovir or valgan cyclovir  let's not forget that this is a drug  that has significant toxicities  um upwards of a third of babies may  develop clinically meaningful  neutropenia on long-term valid  encyclopedia therapy  there's a cost associated with the  frequent laboratory monitoring that's  required for safety  and we  need to be be mindful of that now there  are other drugs in the pipeline  le tamrovir for example has been  licensed for  prophylaxis prophylaxis against cmv  disease and transplant patients  i think that we  um  don't know anything about it really in  infants  but maybe in the future we will see uh  better better um  tests available for  um antiviral drugs uh looking at the  sensitivity of the virus  and um  the need for the reduced need for money  safety monitoring  but right now i think that asymptomatic  babies with congenital cmv should not be  treated with antivirals  great thank you  next question here if a screen comes  back positive from the state laboratory  for cmv can you assume that the baby has  congenital cmv infection or is  additional testing needed if you need  additional tests how soon do they have  to be performed  yes and so as i alluded to i mean yes  you definitely need a confirmatory test  and so in this way it's no different  than hypothyroidism or another  screenable disorder  in the newborn panel and my  recommendation is a urine test that  should be obtained prior to 21 days of  age we we cannot  conclusively make the diagnosis of  congenital cb on a positive screen alone  that's why it's a screening test  thank you next question here what were  the key elements to be able to get  universal screening for congenital cmv  tests approved in minnesota even though  it's not on our usp list  yeah i guess in a word  moms  you know um lobbying in in force and  fashion um  even though i am not much of a expert at  all on social media  um  moms are and they use facebook and other  platforms to  very nicely  mobilize people we had a rally  actually out in front of my lab last  spring  we had legislators attend it was really  just the grassroots lobbying and one of  the things that made it really fun is  that this all happened without  endorsements of  formal thought leader bodies  we we didn't have commercial sponsors it  was all grassroots lobbying by parents  and families it's really democracy in  action  and for those of us who still believe in  democracy in  the united states it was very refreshing  great thank you  we have time for a few more questions  here so this next one asks when talking  about cmv and organ transplantation  i had heard that there is a great  percentage of people that were  unknowingly positive for the cmv  antibody does this say most people have  been exposed to the virus and that they  carry an inactive form of it  yeah so a good question  i would say it means most people are  infected not just exposed they're  infected and i think probably 5 7 of the  world's population  today has cmv infection  most people in all of human history who  have had cmv  probably got it from breastfeeding  mother's milk  and most people are asymptomatic  in  the  hundreds and hundreds of  moms i've interviewed over the years  very very few of women have ever relayed  a specific ailment during their  pregnancy that  made me think of cmv or made me think  that they had a symptomatic cmv  infection mostly in the infections in  pregnancy or asymptomatic  the other important point in that  question and i think the  person who asked it for bringing this up  is cmv infections congenital main  infections actually occur in women who  have long standing immunity  and so that's the great enigma of cmv is  having had infection  does not protect you for life against  re-infection  it's quite fascinating the parallels  with covet 19 where we're having these  same discussions strain variants new  strains emerging women can get  reinfected during pregnancy even if  they're seropositive  so that's why universal screening really  is a  paradigm that should be  exercised for all women irrespective of  the serologic status  it's a debate  worthy of a whole other webinar whether  or not all pregnant women should be  screened for cmv antibodies  but what i've argued is that whether  you're cmv seronegative or sero-positive  you are at risk for either acquiring cmv  primary infection or re-infection during  pregnancy  and transmitting that to um  your baby  and so that's why universal screening of  the newborn i think is really important  now you're less likely to have a  symptomatic or damaged baby  if it's a re-infection  but still 15 or 20 percent of those  babies go on to have hearing loss and so  you can't rely on the presence or  absence of symptoms at birth that's why  universal screening is important  thank you  next question here what are the positive  and negative predictive accuracies of  dbs screening for  ccmv  yeah  great question uh  the positive  predictive value um  and and for that matter the negative  predictive value are both in the 90  percent range  and so we we we didn't see any um  surprises there  um we we  know that the positive predictive value  of any test depends on the prevalence  uh in that population being studied um  you know the reason it was not 99.9  uh is we do have some false positives  with saliva  but the dried blood spot on the other  hand um very few false positives so um i  think the predictive values at least in  our study were quite robust  well it looks like we have time for one  more question here so we'll wrap up with  this one and it asks what specimen type  and testing platform are being used in  ontario for universal screening  yeah so the  um  the ontario platform  is a real-time pcr platform being drawn  down on dried blood spots  and  the  um  primers and  amplification conditions have not  actually been published yet  for those that are interested there will  be a  workshop in canada congenital cmv  meeting the last week of august  and so  for those that would be interested in a  deeper dive  it's a great meeting should be very good  meeting  for general cmv with a lot of  information on screening and i think  we'll hear from the ontario group  there's currently work in progress to  add screening in saskatchewan and a  couple of other provinces  but basically the the platform is  real-time pcr and it's also being done  in conjunction with screening for  connexin  mutations gjb2 the  gene product that's the most common  genetic cause of deafness in north  america uh and so we look forward to  seeing the ontario data published and  comparing notes um with them  i think that'll be extremely valuable  great well thank you again dr schleich  for your time today and your important  research we would also like to thank lab  roots and our sponsor roche diagnostics  for underwriting today's educational  webcast before we go i'd like to thank  the audience for joining us today and  for their interesting questions  questions we do not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand and  lab roots will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event until next time take care  everyone goodbye  you

